WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:06.458
I’m here today as a guest

00:00:06.458 --> 00:00:10.958
on land that was stolen from the Ute,
Cheyenne and Arapaho Nations.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.125
Land that many other tribal nations
thought of as home

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:18.583
before those relationships
were written over by settler colonialism.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.458
So land acknowledgments like this
have become pretty commonplace --

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.875
at the beginning of events,
at universities,

00:00:25.875 --> 00:00:27.667
sometimes in our email signatures.

00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:30.958
But I've always found them
to be kind of confusing.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.625
Once you admit something is stolen,
aren’t you supposed to give it back?

00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:35.666
(Laughter)

00:00:35.708 --> 00:00:37.208
So if there’s anyone listening,

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:40.708
who has a couple of hundred spare acres
that you’re feeling guilty about,

00:00:40.708 --> 00:00:42.750
just contact your local tribal government.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.041
We would be happy to relieve you
of that burden.

00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:46.208
(Laughter)

00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:48.375
That’s probably not you, right?

00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:52.541
Maybe you’ve heard of this movement
to return land to Indigenous people.

00:00:52.541 --> 00:00:55.000
But you're thinking,
"I can barely afford rent.

00:00:55.041 --> 00:00:57.500
What is it I'm supposed
to be giving back?"

00:00:58.375 --> 00:01:00.333
That's what I want to clarify today.

00:01:00.375 --> 00:01:05.458
Because not only is getting land back
in Indigenous hands in your best interests

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.875
and the best interest of the land itself,

00:01:07.875 --> 00:01:10.333
there's ways anyone can help
make it happen.

00:01:10.375 --> 00:01:13.917
I'm a descendant of the Turtle Mountain
Band of Chippewa Indians,

00:01:13.958 --> 00:01:17.583
which is part of the Anishinaabe
Nation of the Great Lakes region.

00:01:18.042 --> 00:01:21.000
Home for me is also the Pacific Northwest.

00:01:21.042 --> 00:01:23.875
I grew up on Kalapuya
and Molalla territory,

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:27.292
and now I'm a professor
of Indigenous studies at CSU.

00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:32.917
My ancestors worked very hard
to navigate a complex and changing world

00:01:32.917 --> 00:01:35.500
that was ravaged by settler colonialism.

00:01:36.583 --> 00:01:38.958
My grandma and both of her parents

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.708
survived the trauma and abuse
of the Indian boarding school system.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.458
The generation before them

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.667
fought to keep our tribe
from being terminated

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:49.500
by the federal government.

00:01:50.542 --> 00:01:52.042
Going even further back,

00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:54.625
my six times great grandfather's signature

00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:58.292
is on the 1863 Treaty of Old Crossing,

00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:03.458
which forced the Anishinaabe to give up
11 million acres of what’s now Minnesota

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.792
in exchange for a little
over 400,000 dollars

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:09.583
and a 640-acre reservation.

00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:12.958
That works out
to about five cents an acre,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.750
which has to be one
of the worst land deals in US history.

00:02:18.458 --> 00:02:20.625
Or maybe the best,
depending on who you ask.

00:02:21.917 --> 00:02:25.708
This sort of thing happened
all over the US and Canada.

00:02:26.417 --> 00:02:28.875
The problem isn't just
that settlers showed up

00:02:28.917 --> 00:02:30.458
and took the land,

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.500
it's how they've treated it ever since.

00:02:33.042 --> 00:02:34.833
That land we lost in Minnesota,

00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:38.833
the Enbridge's Line 3 pipeline
goes right through the middle of it.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.583
That freaking pipeline is responsible
for the largest inland oil spill

00:02:44.625 --> 00:02:46.333
ever recorded in the US.

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:49.708
And now they're trying to expand it.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:54.458
Whether we're talking pipelines
or some other industry,

00:02:54.458 --> 00:02:58.333
the colonial mindset has been
about extracting resources,

00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:01.583
mostly with the goal of making
the rich richer.

00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:05.917
But that's why Landback
is not about Indigenous people

00:03:05.917 --> 00:03:07.833
trying to run a real estate scam.

00:03:08.292 --> 00:03:09.500
We're doing this

00:03:09.542 --> 00:03:11.833
because the land itself is in crisis.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:14.458
Every Indigenous culture is unique,

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.292
but our shared philosophy
is that we come from the land

00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:19.292
and the land is what sustains us.

00:03:19.292 --> 00:03:22.667
And therefore we have
a responsibility to care for it.

00:03:23.875 --> 00:03:30.542
(Applause)

00:03:31.042 --> 00:03:35.208
Landback is about reasserting
Indigenous relationships with the land.

00:03:35.625 --> 00:03:39.375
Relationships that are based on tens
of thousands of years

00:03:39.375 --> 00:03:42.417
of hands-on experience
taking care of our homelands.

00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:46.333
If you've ever tried your hand
at farming or gardening,

00:03:46.375 --> 00:03:50.708
you know that land management takes more
than just showing up with good intentions.

00:03:51.458 --> 00:03:55.792
Globally, Indigenous people are really
good at managing for biodiversity

00:03:55.792 --> 00:03:57.542
and resilient ecosystems.

00:03:58.375 --> 00:04:00.792
That's because we've had
generation upon generation

00:04:00.792 --> 00:04:03.583
to test out what works and what doesn't.

00:04:03.583 --> 00:04:06.458
There's tons of evidence
and examples to back this up.

00:04:06.458 --> 00:04:08.792
One recent study shows
that Indigenous people

00:04:08.792 --> 00:04:11.917
make up just five percent
of the global population,

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:14.542
but we’re managing nearly
half the areas on Earth

00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:16.458
that are protected for conservation

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.792
or still support intact ecosystems.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.500
In the United States, tribal nations
have reintroduced endangered species

00:04:24.542 --> 00:04:26.875
even when the government
said it wouldn't work.

00:04:26.875 --> 00:04:29.083
So in the northwest where I grew up,

00:04:29.125 --> 00:04:31.042
most of the big rivers have been dammed,

00:04:31.083 --> 00:04:33.458
which makes it super hard
for salmon to survive

00:04:33.458 --> 00:04:35.583
and many runs have gone extinct.

00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:37.667
So back in the '90s,

00:04:37.708 --> 00:04:39.792
the Nimiipuu people
told the state of Idaho,

00:04:39.792 --> 00:04:43.125
"Hey, we'd like to bring Coho salmon
back to the Snake River."

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:46.167
The state Fish and Wildlife guys
were like, "I don't think so."

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.167
But the tribe did it anyway.

00:04:48.208 --> 00:04:51.458
They got eggs that one
of the hatcheries was going to throw away,

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.625
incubated them and basically snuck
the fish back into the river.

00:04:56.458 --> 00:04:58.167
And now they're doing so well,

00:04:58.208 --> 00:05:01.083
that the state has reopened
the sport fishery.

00:05:01.125 --> 00:05:04.792
And the tribe is reintroducing Coho
to a bunch of other rivers.

00:05:05.792 --> 00:05:07.125
Same thing with buffalo.

00:05:07.125 --> 00:05:09.958
Back in the 1800s,
when the railroads were going in

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.500
and native people were literally
being kicked off the land at gunpoint,

00:05:13.542 --> 00:05:15.375
buffalo were nearly exterminated

00:05:15.417 --> 00:05:18.583
because they thought that would
make it easier to subdue the tribes

00:05:18.625 --> 00:05:20.042
who depended on them.

00:05:20.042 --> 00:05:21.208
So get this.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.125
Most of the buffalo
that you see today in zoos

00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:25.792
or wildlife reserves

00:05:25.833 --> 00:05:28.250
are actually descended
from conservation herds

00:05:28.292 --> 00:05:30.625
that native people protected back then.

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:34.292
And now the Blackfeet Nation
is bringing free-ranging buffalo

00:05:34.292 --> 00:05:36.333
back to their homeland in Montana.

00:05:36.792 --> 00:05:39.583
We're also pretty good at cleaning up
the ecological messes

00:05:39.625 --> 00:05:41.208
caused by colonialism.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:42.958
The town of Eureka, California,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.417
was like, "OK, we're ready
to give some land back.

00:05:45.458 --> 00:05:48.000
We know it's culturally
really important place to you.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.000
Oh, and by the way, it's a Superfund site,
so it's hella polluted.

00:05:52.042 --> 00:05:53.208
Good luck with that."

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:54.375
(Laughter)

00:05:54.667 --> 00:05:57.583
And the Wiyot tribe said,
"Great, we'll take it."

00:05:57.583 --> 00:06:01.000
And now, they're in the process
of successfully remediating the site.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.750
They've removed tons of trash
and contaminated soil.

00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:11.042
(Applause)

00:06:11.458 --> 00:06:14.500
They're working on erosion control
and wildlife habitat

00:06:14.542 --> 00:06:17.708
and making it a place where they can
hold ceremonies again.

00:06:18.542 --> 00:06:22.333
The land is better off
in Indigenous hands

00:06:22.375 --> 00:06:24.792
because we treat the land
like it's a relative.

00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:30.375
(Applause)

00:06:30.417 --> 00:06:32.125
Even our youth know how this works.

00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:33.958
So I run a summer camp for native kids

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.250
where we talk about Indigenous science
and how we relate to the land.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.500
So last summer, after the camp wraps up,
Henry's mom calls me.

00:06:40.542 --> 00:06:42.500
He's one of the campers, he's six.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:45.208
And she says he's had a falling out
with his best friend.

00:06:45.958 --> 00:06:47.208
They were playing outside,

00:06:47.208 --> 00:06:51.000
and the friend, who's not native,
was squishing grasshoppers for fun.

00:06:51.792 --> 00:06:54.667
Henry tried to stop him,
and the friend said,

00:06:54.708 --> 00:06:57.000
"They're just bugs. It doesn't matter."

00:06:57.042 --> 00:06:59.458
But Henry told him,
"They're not just bugs.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.833
There are our relatives
and you can't kill them."

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.292
So that was the summer I had to learn

00:07:04.333 --> 00:07:06.500
how to keep grasshoppers
from eating my garden

00:07:06.542 --> 00:07:07.708
with garlic spray,

00:07:07.708 --> 00:07:09.792
because every time
I went to use pesticides,

00:07:09.792 --> 00:07:12.375
or something that would kill them,
I thought of Henry

00:07:12.417 --> 00:07:14.208
and what kind of future we might have

00:07:14.208 --> 00:07:16.333
if we actually lived like that.

00:07:18.583 --> 00:07:22.375
Landback is about radically rethinking
how we relate to the land

00:07:22.417 --> 00:07:23.792
and other living beings.

00:07:24.542 --> 00:07:26.958
So look up whose land you're on.

00:07:26.958 --> 00:07:29.833
Some tribes, like the Ohlone and Duwamish,

00:07:29.875 --> 00:07:33.208
have a way for you to pay
a voluntary land tax on their website

00:07:33.208 --> 00:07:36.083
as a way to recognize
that you're a guest on their homeland.

00:07:36.083 --> 00:07:38.167
Way easier than dealing with the IRS.

00:07:39.417 --> 00:07:42.500
Or maybe you do own land,
but you need to live there for now.

00:07:42.958 --> 00:07:45.000
You can set up a bequest in your will

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.042
so that your estate
goes back to the tribe.

00:07:48.083 --> 00:07:50.417
I'm not saying that any of this is easy.

00:07:50.833 --> 00:07:53.292
Unsettling is hard work,

00:07:53.292 --> 00:07:57.833
especially in places where Indigenous
people were removed or erased.

00:07:59.083 --> 00:08:03.875
That absence tends to be papered over
with the myth that colonialism worked.

00:08:03.917 --> 00:08:05.167
Native people disappeared,

00:08:05.208 --> 00:08:07.083
so there's nothing to worry about.

00:08:07.792 --> 00:08:11.167
But when I look
at my own family's history,

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:16.208
the government that took our land
and outlawed our ceremonies,

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:19.208
the boarding schools
that took our language,

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.583
the epidemic of sexual violence

00:08:21.583 --> 00:08:24.667
that has touched nearly every
native woman in my family --

00:08:25.583 --> 00:08:30.708
all of these concerted efforts to end
the story of us as Anishinaabe people.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.292
And yet despite all that,
we're still here.

00:08:35.292 --> 00:08:39.208
Our survival as Indigenous
people is a miracle.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:42.708
And the point of miracles
is that they inspire action.

00:08:43.666 --> 00:08:46.833
I think if we're honest as human beings,

00:08:46.833 --> 00:08:49.125
we know that this path we're on

00:08:49.166 --> 00:08:51.000
is both morally untenable

00:08:51.041 --> 00:08:52.958
and ecologically unsustainable.

00:08:54.208 --> 00:08:56.458
Landback is a call to action.

00:08:57.583 --> 00:09:00.416
To get real that the current system

00:09:00.416 --> 00:09:04.000
is only really working for an increasingly
smaller group of folks.

00:09:05.375 --> 00:09:08.875
But when the people who are benefiting
from this legacy of stolen land

00:09:08.875 --> 00:09:10.291
step forward,

00:09:10.333 --> 00:09:12.083
real change can happen.

00:09:12.541 --> 00:09:16.125
This isn't just an opportunity
to right a past injustice.

00:09:16.458 --> 00:09:18.541
It's the only way to heal the land itself.

00:09:19.208 --> 00:09:22.583
And that means
a better future for everyone.

00:09:23.208 --> 00:09:24.416
Thank you.

00:09:24.416 --> 00:09:26.041
(Applause)

